on the only  mechanism available is sn2 even with  these hindered nucleophiles this is  really very unhindered so we're going to  form a bond next let's consider a  primary substrate that is unhindered  primary substrates don't tend to react  very well with these poor nucleophiles  either so will predict no reaction for  this first row again now primary carbo  cations are not stable either so with  weak bases we're going to get sn2 again  now being unhindered like this even with  a strong base we're going to get  substitution here in fact if X is a  halide and low R is say sodium methoxide  that's the Williamson ether synthesis  reaction now it's only when we use a  strong hindered base that attack right  here in an sn2 fashion is not as  possible we're going to have steric  hindrance from the bulkiness of the base  and so when we treat this substrate with  a strong hindered base we get  elimination again carbo cations primary  carbo cations are not stable so this  elimination will be an e2 mechanism now  let's consider a hindered primary  substrate so we have a little bit of  branching over here this is still a  primary substrate so with a poor  nucleophile we're going to again expect  no reaction and again with our weakly  basic nucleophiles we'll expect  sn2 now these strong bases are not going  to have as easy access to this carbon to  kick off the leaving group in an sn2  fashion like they do with the unbranched  primary so here we'll see when we start  using strong base and strong hindered  base we're going to promote e to  eliminations with the substrate of  course it's e to because we're still  dealing with a primary substrate primary  carbo cations are not stable let's move  on to secondary substrates now now we're  getting into the realm of where stable  carbo cation  and conform so when we have a poor  nucleophile which you should notice is  actually also a polar protic solvent  acetic acid water alcohols this is when  we're going to start getting some sn1  reactions going on the nucleophile isn't  strong it doesn't have a lot of pushing  power to push out this group in an sn2  fashion so under these conditions we'll  just wait around for this to ionize and  then the weak nucleophile can add now  whenever we're able to form a carbo  cation elimination is going to compete  so under these conditions we'll expect  to get some elimination by the e1  mechanism as well now we add some  negative charge a little bit more  pushing power into our nucleophile and  the main mechanism when we treat these  with a weak base our secondary  substrates is going to be sn2 now if we  compare our secondary substrate to our  primary hindered substrate we see we  have a similar type of branching over  here that branching is going to  sterically hindered  at the leaving group but the adjacent  protons are quite available and so  strong bases are going to promote  elimination these are really good  nucleophiles and they're going to push  our leaving group out by an e2 mechanism  in both our strong base and strong  hindered base situations onto our  tertiary substrates in order for an sn2  reaction to happen we need to get  backside attack of the nucleophile  displacing the leaving group tertiary is  much too hindered for that to be  possible and so under our poor  nucleophile conditions we're going to  wait around for this to ionize and then  the poor nucleophile can add in and this  describes an sn1 mechanism again once we  form the carbo cation here it's pretty  easy to get elimination and so there's a  competing mechanism under these  conditions which is again II one weak  bases will also promote a mixture of sn1  and e1 products a  the strong base and strong hindered  based situations don't provide the polar  protic environment that we need for this  compound to ionize and so each of these  situations is going to promote  elimination again by the e2 mechanism  okay let's do a summary of the trends  we're starting to discover from this  chart all of our primary and our methyl  case they do not react well with weak  nucleophiles so we've this corner of our  chart which doesn't react once  substitution mechanisms become available  with primary substrates we're going to  get sn2 as the substrate becomes more  hindered e 2 becomes a factor and strong  hindered bases whenever possible promote  e 2 and that actually extends all the  way down into this corner of the chart  with our secondary and tertiary  substrates so that's a good general  trend to notice once we can form stable  secondary and tertiary carbo cations the  sn1 mechanism becomes a choice under  these polar protic conditions with these  weak nucleophiles the substrates going  to sit around until it ionizes and then  the weak nucleophile can come in and  attack that describes an sn1 mechanism  an e1 competes with that secondary  substrates are only about as hindered as  this primary branched example and so  they'll undergo sn2 when treated with a  weak base but the tertiary substrate  cannot do that we can't get backside  attack so even under weakly basic  conditions we have the sn1 and e1  mechanisms competing here again our  strong bases and strong hindered bases  are promoting e2 reactions except when  that mechanism is not available and when  this primary substrate is completely  unhindered with the strong base I hope  this video is helping you start to cope  with all of these competing mechanisms  and start to see that there actually are  general trends based on our substrate  and our nucleophile strengths and  properties in the next video in this  series I'm going to continue this chart  to include a  lilyc and benzylic substrates these are  much more complicated due to resonance  effects and electronic effects through  the Rings so if you'd like to go a  little bit deeper please come back and  see me again  subscribe hit that notification bell and  we'll start to talk about those  substrates next time
 

